## 3. Common pitfalls that trigger the exact error you saw

| Symptom | Why it happens | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| **You’re calling `/v1/customers` with raw card fields** (`card[number]`, etc.) | Those fields are **not** part of the `Customer` endpoint; they only belong to the **Token** endpoint (`/v1/tokens`). Stripe therefore sees no `source`/`customer` parameter. | Use Stripe.js (`stripe.createToken` or `stripe.createPaymentMethod`) first. |
| **You send a token but use the wrong field name** (`token: "tok_..."` instead of `source`) | The Customer API expects the token under the key `source` (legacy) or `payment_method` (new). | Rename the JSON key accordingly, or map it on the server. |
| **Creating a Charge while passing only a Customer that has no default source** | The Customer was created without a card, so Stripe can’t bill it. | Either (a) attach a source to the Customer first, **or** pass a token/PM directly in the Charge request (`source: "tok_..."`). |
| **Using the newest API version but still using `source`** | From Stripe API v2020‑08‑27 onward, `source` works for legacy cards but `payment_method` is preferred for SCA‑compatible flows. | Switch to `payment_method` and set `invoice_settings.default_payment_method`. |
| **Test mode vs. live mode mismatch** | You generated a token with a **test** publishable key but sent it to a **live** secret key (or vice‑versa). | Ensure both client and server use keys from the same environment. |
| **Missing `email` or other required top‑level field** (rare) | Some libraries (e.g., older SDKs) enforce a minimum set of fields; missing data can cascade into a “source missing” error. | Provide at least `email` or `description` to avoid validation shortcuts. |

