Stripe is rejecting the call because the parameter you’re sending is not one of the values the /v1/customers endpoint will accept.  
Since mid-2019 the Customers API no longer lets you pass raw card data (card[number], card[exp_month] …) directly.  
You must give it one of the following instead:

• source (tok_123… or card_123…) – a token or source object that already represents the card.  
• payment_method (pm_123…) – a saved PaymentMethod you created earlier.  
• customer – when you’re re-using an existing customer’s default payment source.

If none of those parameters is present you get the error you’re seeing:

“Invalid request: must provide source or customer id.”

Why the card fields you send are ignored  
Stripe treats any unrecognised parameter as “extra” and silently discards it; then it checks whether a source or customer was provided. Because the card fields are no longer recognised, the request looks empty to Stripe and it returns the error.

How to fix it

1. Collect the card details on the client (browser / mobile) with Stripe-JS, Stripe Elements, or a mobile SDK.  
   Example with Elements (browser JS):
   ```js
   const {token, error} = await stripe.createToken(cardElement);
   if (error) { /* show the error to your customer */ }
   fetch('/create-customer', {  
     method: 'POST',
     body: JSON.stringify({ token: token.id, email })  
   });
   ```

2. Send only the token (or PaymentMethod id) to your backend.  
   Example with the Stripe Node library:
   ```js
   // req.body.token is “tok_xxx” coming from step 1
   const customer = await stripe.customers.create({
     email: req.body.email,
     name:  req.body.name,
     source: req.body.token          // <- this is what Stripe expects
   });
   ```

   If you prefer PaymentMethod / Setup Intents:
   ```js
   // From the client you call stripe.createPaymentMethod({type:'card', card:cardElement})
   const paymentMethod = req.body.paymentMethod; // “pm_xxx”

   const customer = await stripe.customers.create({ email:req.body.email });
   await stripe.paymentMethods.attach(paymentMethod, {customer: customer.id});
   await stripe.customers.update(customer.id, {
     invoice_settings: { default_payment_method: paymentMethod }
   });
   ```

3. Never send raw card data to your server.  
   Besides failing with the new API version, this breaks PCI-DSS compliance because your server would be touching card data directly.

